  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Benzene reacts with concentrated nitric acid at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>323</mn><mo>-333 </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> in the presence of concentrated sulphuric acid to form nitrobenzene. This converts to Aniline on reaction with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Sn</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>HCl</mi></math>.</p><p>The chemical process used in converting a primary aromatic amine into the corresponding diazonium salt of the amine is commonly referred to as diazotization by using sodium nitrite and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HCl</mi></math>. This on reaction with phenol leads to diazotisation coupling.</p><p> </p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/a2870f47-88a7-4bac-b6a8-c9452676f954.png\" style=\"width: 700px; height: 349px;\"/></p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
